---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-to-calculate-overtime-pay"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    Overtime pay is your regular rate multiplied by an overtime rate — usually
    time and a half — for the hours over a threshold. The arithmetic is simple;
    the parts that need care are the <em>regular rate</em> and the
    <em>threshold</em>, both of which depend on your situation and location.
  </p>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p>
    <code>overtime rate = regular hourly rate × multiplier</code><br />
    <code>overtime pay = overtime rate × overtime hours</code><br />
    <code>gross pay = regular hours × regular rate + overtime pay</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    Example: $20 an hour, 46 hours in the week, US federal rule. 40 hours at $20
    is $800. 6 overtime hours at $30 (1.5 × $20) is $180. Gross pay is
    <strong>$980</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What "time and a half" means</h2>
  <p>
    1.5 times the regular rate. On $18 an hour that is $27. "Double time" is 2×
    ($36). Some awards and contracts use other multipliers.
  </p>

  <h2>The regular rate is not always your hourly wage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salaried non-exempt:</strong> divide the weekly salary by the hours it covers to get the regular rate, then apply the formula.</li>
    <li><strong>Two pay rates in one week:</strong> the regular rate is the weighted average of the rates, based on hours at each.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-discretionary bonuses and commission:</strong> under US federal law these are added into the regular rate for the weeks they cover, which raises the overtime rate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Thresholds by location</h2>
  <p>These are calculation starting points, <strong>not legal advice</strong> — your entitlement depends on your employee classification, award or contract, and current law.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Overtime threshold</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>US — federal (FLSA)</td><td>Over 40 hours / week</td><td>1.5×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>US — California</td><td>Over 8 hours / day or 40 / week; over 12 / day</td><td>1.5× then 2×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canada — Ontario</td><td>Over 44 hours / week</td><td>1.5×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canada — federal</td><td>Over 40 hours / week</td><td>1.5×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>No statutory overtime rate — set by contract</td><td>Contractual</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Australia</td><td>Set by the relevant modern award (often 38-hour week)</td><td>Award-based, often 1.5× then 2×</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The UK's 48-hour Working Time limit caps <em>average</em> weekly hours for
    health and safety; it does not require any overtime pay.
  </p>

  <h2>Do salaried employees get overtime?</h2>
  <p>
    Often not. Under the FLSA, employees classified as <em>exempt</em> — which
    generally requires both a salary above a set level and certain job duties —
    are not entitled to overtime. <em>Non-exempt</em> employees are, whether they
    are paid hourly or by salary.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculators</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/calculators/overtime-calculator/">Overtime Calculator</a> — enter your rate and hours, pick a rule or set your own.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> — a full week of shifts with the overtime split applied.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/hourly-pay-calculator/">Hourly Pay Calculator</a> — gross pay projections including overtime.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/overtime" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor, Wage and Hour Division — Overtime Pay</a> (Fair Labor Standards Act)</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_overtime.htm" rel="noopener">California Department of Industrial Relations — Overtime</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.ontario.ca/document/your-guide-employment-standards-act-0/overtime" rel="noopener">Ontario — Employment Standards Act: Overtime</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/overtime-your-rights" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — Overtime: your rights</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.fairwork.gov.au/pay-and-wages/penalty-rates-and-allowances/overtime-pay" rel="noopener">Fair Work Ombudsman — Overtime pay</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology &amp; Sources</a> page for how these rules are applied in the calculator.</p>
</GuideLayout>
